#heading(numbering: none)[Abstract]
A mind that is grown is more robust and more individual than one that is
configured. IPSE (Interoceptive Predictive Self-Emergence; Latin _ipse_, "itself")
is a developmental scaffold for growing one on a Booster K1 humanoid. The body's
internal state — battery, joint heat, motor current, balance — enters as
observation, not reward; one predictive objective, expected free energy, is meant
to yield drives, curiosity, preference and dissent; and the self is not specified
but measured, as a behavior-invariant representation that persists while skills
change. We describe the scaffold as built: pure organs on a typed bus that run
identically live, from tape, in simulation and in sleep, behind one body interface
shared by a K1 and a Unitree G1. Organs are scaffolding, to be dissolved into
learned networks as data accrues; a small innate brainstem is not. We pre-register
a self-probe (E0) and three supporting claims: prediction error replaces dedicated
detectors (E1), surprise-prioritized sleep improves the next day (E2), and
inherited networks shorten a new body's development (E3). _Results: TBD._
